 "subject": "Changes in lp__ for a structural VAR model",
 "messages_listed": 7,
 "last_activity": "2013-07-20T04:12:15Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "ZOhcbSOsT4gJ",
   "from": "Carlos",
   "author": "fde4048c840328bf",
   "date": "2013-07-20T00:08:27Z",
   "subject": "Changes in lp__ for a structural VAR model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hi!<div><br></div><div>I am trying to estimate with STAN a structural Vector Autoregressive model. In order to do it, I think that I have to modify the lp__ in the model block, but I am not sure if I am doing it properly. It seems to work, but I would like some confirmation that I am on the right track if that&#39;s possible. I attach a (half-page long) pdf with the likelihood of the model, an R file that generates data, and the STAN file (very preliminary) Everything is very short, I promise! </div><div><br></div><div>What I have done is to correct the log of a multivariate normal by the determinant of matrix A.</div><div><br></div><div>The idea is that matrix A has some restrictions that I need to impose in the estimation, so I need to take some care of it. </div><div><br></div><div>Thanks!</div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "sP335hBh7qYJ",
   "from": "Jiqiang Guo",
   "author": "ab7b24da45678eac",
   "date": "2013-07-20T02:30:35Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Changes in lp__ for a structural VAR model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "I do not see a problem with the approach though log(|*|) instead of 0.5 * log(|*|^2) in the Stan code would be a bit better.   <div><br></div><div>--</div><div>Jiqiang <br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Fri, Jul 19, 2013 at 8:08 PM, Carlos <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">car...@montesgaldon.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\r\n\r\n</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">Hi!<div><br></div><div>I am trying to estimate with STAN a structural Vector Autoregressive model. In order to do it, I think that I have to modify the lp__ in the model block, but I am not sure if I am doing it properly. It seems to work, but I would like some confirmation that I am on the right track if that&#39;s possible. I attach a (half-page long) pdf with the likelihood of the model, an R file that generates data, and the STAN file (very preliminary) Everything is very short, I promise! </div>\r\n\r\n<div><br></div><div>What I have done is to correct the log of a multivariate normal by the determinant of matrix A.</div><div><br></div><div>The idea is that matrix A has some restrictions that I need to impose in the estimation, so I need to take some care of it. </div>\r\n\r\n<div><br></div><div>Thanks!</div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><span><font color=\"#888888\">\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;stan users mailing list&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">stan-users+...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792468016125000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3RtVUgFXbxkfbLEQdPvw-f\">https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out</a>.<br>\r\n <br>\r\n <br>\r\n</font></span></blockquote></div><br></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "vn_ovOjcY2oJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2013-07-20T02:32:01Z",
   "subject": "Re: Changes in lp__ for a structural VAR model",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "On Friday, July 19, 2013 8:08:27 PM UTC-4, Carlos wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">I\r\n am trying to estimate with STAN a structural Vector Autoregressive \r\nmodel. In order to do it, I think that I have to modify the lp__ in the \r\nmodel block, but I am not sure if I am doing it properly. It seems to \r\nwork, but I would like some confirmation that I am on the right track if\r\n that&#39;s possible. </blockquote>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div><br>This isn&#39;t optimal:<code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">lp__ </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> lp__ </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">square</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">determinant</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> multi_normal_cholesky_log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">Y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> C </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> B</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> H</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>First,  <code><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">square</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">determinant</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"></span></code> == <code><span style=\"color:#660\"></span><span style=\"color:#000\">log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">_</span><span style=\"color:#000\">determinant</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">) </span></code>and\r\n the log_determinant() function is something optimized that Marcus \r\nwrote. But in your case, A is diagonal so the log determinant is just \r\nsum(log(A1)) which is much faster than doing anything on an arbitrary \r\npositive definite matrix.<br><br>Second, if H is diagonal, then you don&#39;t want to call any variant of multi_normal(). Instead subtract<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><span style=\"color:#000\">(A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">Y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">) -</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> (C </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> B</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))) ./ H1 )</span><span style=\"color:#660\"></span></code></div><br>Then,\r\n you have to account for the log determinant of H * H&#39; , which is just 2\r\n * sum(log(H1)) but is multiplied by -1/2 in the multinormal \r\nlog-likelihood.<br><br>So, I think it should be<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#606\">lp__ &lt;- lp__ + sum(log(A1)) - </span><code><span style=\"color:#000\">dot_self</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><code><span style=\"color:#000\">(A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">Y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">) -</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> (C </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> B</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))) ./ H1 ) - </span></code></code>sum(log(H1));<span style=\"color:#660\"></span></div></code></div><br>Ben<br></div><div><br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "QlVNYVKqYkwJ",
   "from": "Carlos",
   "author": "fde4048c840328bf",
   "date": "2013-07-20T03:23:12Z",
   "subject": "Re: Changes in lp__ for a structural VAR model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div>Thank you!</div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\">This isn&#39;t optimal:<br><code><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">lp__ </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> lp__ </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">+</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">log</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">square</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">determinant</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">A</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)))</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">+</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> multi_normal_cholesky_log</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">A</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">col</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">Y</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">),</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> C </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">+</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> B</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">col</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">X</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">),</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> H</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">);</span></code></blockquote><div><br></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "That&#39;s what I thought...I am not used yet to do this changes directly in STAN. "
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\">First,  <code><span style=\"color:rgb(0,102,102)\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">log</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">square</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">determinant</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">A</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">)))</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"></span></code> == <code><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\"></span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">log</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">_</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">determinant</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">A</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">) </span></code>and the log_determinant() function is something optimized that Marcus wrote.</blockquote><div><br></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div>Stupid things that one misses after 10 hours of work. I saw the log_determinant function, but I thought I couldn&#39;t use it because of the square... Yes, shoot me.</div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\">But in your case, A is diagonal so the log determinant is just sum(log(A1)) which is much faster than doing anything on an arbitrary positive definite matrix.</blockquote><div><br></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div>Mmmmm nope, A is not diagonal. I create first an identity matrix, but then I assign also some values in the off-diagonal cells. The idea is that A in general is not diagonal. The off-diagonal values represent the restrictions on the contemporaneous relationships among the variables in y.</div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\"><br>Second, if H is diagonal, then you don&#39;t want to call any variant of multi_normal(). Instead subtract<br><code><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">(A</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">col</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">Y</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">) -</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> (C </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">+</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> B</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">col</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">X</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">))) ./ H1 )</span></code><br>Then, you have to account for the log determinant of H * H&#39; , which is just 2 * sum(log(H1)) but is multiplied by -1/2 in the multinormal log-likelihood.<br>So, I think it should be<br><code><span style=\"color:rgb(102,0,102)\">lp__ &lt;- lp__ + sum(log(A1)) - </span><code><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">dot_self</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> </span><code><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">(A</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">col</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">Y</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">) -</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> (C </span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">+</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"> B</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">*</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">col</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">(</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">X</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">,</span><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(102,102,0)\">))) ./ H1 ) - </span></code></code>sum(log(H1));</code></blockquote><div><code><br></code></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div>Ok, I understand how you get this! It makes sense! And yes, it looks much more efficient!</div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><br><div><br></div><div><br><div><br>On Friday, July 19, 2013 10:32:01 PM UTC-4, Ben Goodrich wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">On Friday, July 19, 2013 8:08:27 PM UTC-4, Carlos wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">I\r\n am trying to estimate with STAN a structural Vector Autoregressive \r\nmodel. In order to do it, I think that I have to modify the lp__ in the \r\nmodel block, but I am not sure if I am doing it properly. It seems to \r\nwork, but I would like some confirmation that I am on the right track if\r\n that&#39;s possible. </blockquote><div><br>This isn&#39;t optimal:<code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">lp__ </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> lp__ </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">square</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">determinant</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> multi_normal_cholesky_log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">Y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> C </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> B</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> H</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>First,  <code><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">square</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">determinant</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"></span></code> == <code><span style=\"color:#660\"></span><span style=\"color:#000\">log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">_</span><span style=\"color:#000\">determinant</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">) </span></code>and\r\n the log_determinant() function is something optimized that Marcus \r\nwrote. But in your case, A is diagonal so the log determinant is just \r\nsum(log(A1)) which is much faster than doing anything on an arbitrary \r\npositive definite matrix.<br><br>Second, if H is diagonal, then you don&#39;t want to call any variant of multi_normal(). Instead subtract<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><span style=\"color:#000\">(A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">Y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">) -</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> (C </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> B</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))) ./ H1 )</span><span style=\"color:#660\"></span></code></div><br>Then,\r\n you have to account for the log determinant of H * H&#39; , which is just 2\r\n * sum(log(H1)) but is multiplied by -1/2 in the multinormal \r\nlog-likelihood.<br><br>So, I think it should be<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#606\">lp__ &lt;- lp__ + sum(log(A1)) - </span><code><span style=\"color:#000\">dot_self</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><code><span style=\"color:#000\">(A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">Y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">) -</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> (C </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> B</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">col</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">X</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))) ./ H1 ) - </span></code></code>sum(log(H1));<span style=\"color:#660\"></span></div></code></div><br>Ben<br></div><div><br></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
