   "parts": [
    [
     3,
     "On Saturday, July 20, 2013 12:00:48 AM UTC-4, Ben Goodrich wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">On Friday, July 19, 2013 11:15:32 PM UTC-4, Carlos wrote:<br><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\">But in your case, A is diagonal so the log determinant is just sum(log(A1)) which is much faster than doing anything on an arbitrary positive definite matrix.</blockquote><div><br></div><div>Mmmmm nope, A is not diagonal. I create first an identity matrix, but then I assign also some values in the off-diagonal cells. The idea is that A in general is not diagonal. The off-diagonal values represent the restrictions on the contemporaneous relationships among the variables in y.</div></blockquote><div><br><div><div><div><div>I said the wrong thing. In your example,<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><pre><span style=\"color:#000\">    </span><span style=\"color:#800\">// Build matrix A</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    A           </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> diag_matrix</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">rep_vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">));</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\">      </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> A1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\">      </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> A1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\">      </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> A1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span></pre></div></code></div><br>A is lower-triangular, in which case its log determinant is still 2 * sum(log(A1)). So, I was wrong to say that it was diagonal but the expression for its log determinant is the same as if it were diagonal. But if in your real problem, A is not triangular, then go ahead and use Marcus&#39; log_determinant() function.<br></div></div></div></div></div></blockquote>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div><br>And if in your real problem the diagonal cells of A are all 1.0, then the log determinant is the constant zero and can just be omitted. But if you have parameters on the diagonal, then you need to account for the log determinant.<br><br>Ben<br><br><div><div><div><div><div><br></div></div></div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
